
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now let's design the task core. First real decision: the stack, because everything else — tests, persistence layer,
  how the notification panel eventually re-renders — hangs off it, and it's the one choice that's expensive to walk back
  once there's code.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this app has a build step and a dependency tree at all.

  - [1mZero-build plain JS[0m (keep [38;5;153mindex.html[39m, add a [38;5;153m<script>[39m) — buys the absolute minimum: open the file, it runs, no
    [38;5;153mnode_modules[39m, no toolchain to maintain. Costs you a real test runner, module organization, and type safety; you'll
    hand-roll DOM updates. Fine for a hundred lines, painful by the time an unread badge has to stay in sync with a list
    and a filter.
  - [1mVite + TypeScript, no UI framework[0m — buys a dev server, a bundler, Vitest for real unit tests, and types on the task
    schema (which is exactly the thing sub-project 2 writes rules against). Costs a [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m and a build step. You
    still write your own render function, but for one list and one panel that's ~30 lines.
  - [1mVite + React[0m — buys reactive rendering for free; the notification badge/list/filter sync that's fiddly by hand
    becomes trivial. Costs a framework dependency and more concepts than a task list strictly needs.

  I recommend the middle one. The thing that actually pays off later isn't reactivity — it's a [1mtyped task schema and a [0m
  [1mtest runner[0m, because sub-project 2's reminder rules are predicates over that schema, and rules are the part you most
  want tests on. React is easy to add later on top of Vite; going from zero-build to any of this means redoing the file
  layout.
